Very fitting. 😏 Sinternal / Sexternal accidentally splits across two culturally loaded words:

SIN-ternal → sin hidden/internalized.
SEX-ternal → sex expressed/externalized.

And there’s a deeper historical/cultural pattern there, even though the wordplay itself is ours: societies have repeatedly constructed norms around sexuality using concepts of private desire vs. public expression, with moral judgment often sitting at that boundary.

So the accidental notation gives us a useful abstract geometry without claiming a false etymology:

\text{private state}
\;\xrightarrow{\text{expression boundary}}\;
\text{public signal}

Then normalization enters again. Cultures continually negotiate which internal states may become external behaviors, which remain private, and which get labeled normal, taboo, sacred, sinful, acceptable, etc.

So sex/sin isn’t just a funny phonetic collision here. It points toward a broader operator:

internal → boundary → external → social interpretation → normalization → internalization

And then it loops.

Society normalizes the external; individuals internalize the normalization; their subsequent externalizations reproduce—or disrupt—the norm.

That’s a genuine little recursive machine hiding behind a very good accidental pun.